## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props change. For primitives, "change" means a different value. For objects and functions, it means a different **reference** — even if the contents are identical.

```js
// Every render creates a NEW object reference → child always re-renders
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />

// Every render creates a NEW function reference → child always re-renders
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

This is because `{ color: 'red' } !== { color: 'red' }` in JavaScript — two object literals are never the same reference.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component; skips re-render if props are shallowly equal.
- Use when: a component is pure (output depends only on props) and re-renders are expensive.
- Pointless if props include new object/function references every render (see below).

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a function reference across renders.
- Use when: passing a callback to a memoized child (`React.memo`) or as a `useEffect` dependency.
- Don't use for every function — only when reference stability matters.

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed value across renders.
- Use when: the computation is expensive, **or** you're passing an object/array to a memoized child.
- Don't use for cheap calculations; the overhead of memoization can exceed the savings.

| Tool | Memoizes | Use for |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | Component render | Pure components with stable props |
| `useCallback` | Function reference | Callbacks passed to memoized children |
| `useMemo` | Computed value | Expensive computations or stable object refs |

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer of a context re-renders when the context **value** changes. The common trap: passing a new object literal as the value.

```js
// Bad: new object every render → ALL consumers re-render on every parent render
<UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
```

Fix: memoize the value object.

```js
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user, setUser]);
<UserContext.Provider value={value}>
```

A deeper fix: **split contexts** — separate read context from write context so components that only call setters don't re-render when data changes.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `List` re-renders on every `App` render, even when `items` hasn't changed:

```jsx
function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // New function reference every render
  const handleSelect = (id) => console.log('selected', id);

  // New array reference every render
  const config = { sortBy: 'name', limit: 10 };

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>
      <List items={items} onSelect={handleSelect} config={config} />
    </>
  );
}

const List = ({ items, onSelect, config }) => {
  console.log('List rendered'); // fires on every count increment
  return items.map(i => <Item key={i.id} item={i} onSelect={onSelect} />);
};
```

**After** — `List` skips re-renders when `count` changes:

```jsx
function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // Stable reference across renders
  const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => console.log('selected', id), []);

  // Stable object reference across renders
  const config = useMemo(() => ({ sortBy: 'name', limit: 10 }), []);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>
      <List items={items} onSelect={handleSelect} config={config} />
    </>
  );
}

// Skips render when props are shallowly equal
const List = React.memo(({ items, onSelect, config }) => {
  console.log('List rendered'); // only fires when items/onSelect/config change
  return items.map(i => <Item key={i.id} item={i} onSelect={onSelect} />);
});
```

---

**Key principle**: `React.memo` is only effective when all props have stable references. `useCallback` and `useMemo` exist to provide that stability — they're the prerequisite, not the solution on their own.
